commit 15d82230bc4c19138f91c7cf857c5def75c79bce
Author: ahuzmeza <32289581+ahuzmeza@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Oct 30 06:07:28 2026 +0200

    Parakeet generateStream: stream only text a later merge can't change (#346)
    
    generateStream yields the text that follows the previously emitted text.
    When the next chunk's merge revises the last emitted words, hasPrefix
    fails and the whole transcript is yielded again, so a consumer that
    appends tokens gets it twice. On a 72 s clip (the CLI's 30 s chunks,
    1 s overlap) one chunk ended on "rang", the model wrote "rang.", and
    the next chunk revised it to "rang seven times,".
    
    Both merges keep unchanged the leading run of tokens that end by the
    incoming chunk's start minus the overlap. So after each merge, stream
    only that run for the next chunk; the last chunk streams the rest. The
    streamed text now equals the final .result text. Tokens from the last
    2 s of each chunk are sent with a later chunk (the next one at 5 s
    chunks and up).
    
    parakeet-tdt-0.6b-v3, STT CLI --stream, words streamed before -> after:
      72 s clip, 30 s chunks: 440 -> 242 (final 242)
      88 s clip, 30 s chunks: 261 -> 182 (final 182)
      72 s clip,  5 s chunks: 485 -> 210 (final 210)
      88 s clip,  5 s chunks: 275 -> 151 (final 151)
    Streamed text equals the final text byte for byte in all four, at
    1.5 s chunks and on a 20 s single-chunk clip; final text unchanged.

commit d08558975ef219e5ed38c71fe2522cc03167899f
Author: ahuzmeza <32289581+ahuzmeza@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Oct 29 21:18:53 2026 +0200

    Whisper: fix words lost at the 30 s cuts in long audio (#345)
    
    * Whisper: fix words lost at the 30 s cuts in long audio
    
    Whisper listens to 30 s of audio at a time. For longer audio, the Swift port cut the file at 30 s, 60 s, 90 s… and transcribed each piece on its own, so words that crossed a cut were lost or garbled. A piece that started mid-sentence could come back almost empty: on an 88 s clip, the second piece returned one of its five sentences.
    
    This PR uses the method OpenAI Whisper uses for long audio (`transcribe()` in openai/whisper), which Python mlx-audio's Whisper also follows (`mlx_audio/stt/models/whisper/whisper.py`). Whisper splits what it hears into segments, usually a sentence or a phrase, and marks where each one starts and ends with a timestamp. A 30 s window keeps only the segments that finish inside it, and the next window starts where the last finished segment ended. Speech cut off at the edge of one window is heard again, whole, in the next.
    
    For example, a window comes back as
    
        <|0.00|> The bus was late.<|6.00|><|6.00|> We walked home.<|14.20|><|14.20|> Then it started to ra
    
    The first two segments are kept (0–6.00 s and 6.00–14.20 s). "Then it started to ra" has no end mark, so it is dropped and the next window starts at 14.20 s, where "Then it started to rain" is heard whole.
    
    Edge cases:
    - If a window's last segment has an end mark and nothing follows it, nothing is redone: the next window starts where this window ends.
    - If a window holds only one segment, it is kept whole and the next window starts where this window ends.
    - The loop always moves forward: a segment always ends after it starts, and if the next start would not move, the loop skips a full window ahead.
    
    What else changes for audio over 30 s: each entry in `STTOutput.segments` is one segment with its real start and end time, and streaming sends each segment once it is finished, instead of word by word. Audio up to 30 s works as before; on five such clips the text, segments and token counts are identical. Left out, because the Swift decoder did not have them before either: feeding the previous text back in as context, retrying when the output looks wrong, and skipping silent windows. The audio features are still computed per window, not once for the whole file.
    
    Results (whisper-large-v3-turbo, English, Release build, Mac mini M2 Pro). Word error rate = wrong, missing and extra words per word of the script, lower is better:
    - 72 s clip, 242 words (macOS `say`): 7.0% → 0.8%. Before, the phrases at 30 s and 60 s were missing.
    - 88 s clip, 225 words (5 sentences repeated 3 times): 32.0% → 7.1%.
    - The `mlx-whisper` Python package on the same clips, same method (previous-text context off): 0.8% and 6.7%. The only difference is one extra word at a spot both mishear.
    - Speed on the 88 s clip, including model load, 3 runs: 4.38–4.50 s → 6.02–6.03 s, for one more window and the timestamps.
    
    Tests: where Whisper may place timestamps, and how a window is split and where the next one starts (the normal case, a window ending on a finished segment, a window with one segment). 971 tests pass. The Whisper README's note on long audio now describes this.
    
    * Preserve tokenizer spacing across Whisper segments
    
    ---------
    
    Co-authored-by: Lucas Newman <lucasnewman@me.com>

